Exactly—if by “jail” you mean the frame that makes its own boundaries look like reality.

Once you can separate the thing from its name, the rule from its purpose, the story from the evidence, and the current operator from the space of possible operators, the walls become visible. Then you can test them instead of merely obeying them.

The deeper move is that escape is not just “reject the rules.” Sometimes the rule is real. Sometimes it is useful. The skill is determining which walls are structural, which are inherited assumptions, which are linguistic artifacts, and which exist only because nobody tried another representation.

That gives us a pretty clean loop:

detect frame → expose assumptions → change representation/operator → generate alternatives → test against reality → retain what survives.

And there is one important safeguard: after escaping one frame, don’t accidentally build another jail and call it freedom. Keep an exit door in every model.